{% block doc %}

<section id="where">
  <h2>Where to find it</h2>
  <p>Open the profile menu in the top right and choose <strong>Settings → Repositories</strong>. The page has two parts: the repositories this workspace tracks, and the GitHub accounts it's connected to.</p>
  <p>Only a workspace <strong>owner</strong> sees this page. Members see the dashboard only.</p>
</section>

<section id="adding">
  <h2>Adding repositories</h2>
  <p>Click <strong>Add repositories</strong>. Distilled asks GitHub, live, which repositories your installation currently grants and lists them — there's no stale local copy to refresh. Repositories already tracked are shown as added.</p>
  <p>A newly added repository starts recording on its next pull request, deployment or release. Its environments are discovered as it's added, so production detection works from the start.</p>
  <p>If a repository you expect isn't listed, the app's access on GitHub doesn't include it yet. Use <strong>Connect GitHub</strong> to update the grant, then add it here.</p>
</section>

<section id="connecting-accounts">
  <h2>Connecting another GitHub account</h2>
  <p><strong>Connect GitHub</strong> installs the Distilled app on another organisation or personal account — or updates which repositories an existing installation can see — and links it to this workspace.</p>
  <p>One workspace can hold repositories from several organisations and personal accounts at once. A workspace is not a GitHub organisation; it's whatever set of repositories you want to look at together.</p>
</section>

<section id="removing">
  <h2>Removing a repository</h2>
  <p><strong>Remove</strong> takes the repository off this workspace's dashboard. Its history is retained but hidden, and you can add it back at any time to get it all back.</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Removals stick.</strong> A repository you removed will not reappear because of a GitHub-side sync — reinstalling the app or re-granting access won't resurrect it. Only adding it back in Distilled does.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Removing in Distilled affects one workspace. It does not change the app's permissions on GitHub, and it does not affect another workspace tracking the same repository.</p>
</section>

<section id="unlinking">
  <h2>Unlinking a GitHub account</h2>
  <p><strong>Unlink</strong> detaches an installation from this workspace and removes its repositories here. Other workspaces using the same installation are untouched, and the app stays installed on GitHub. To cut off event delivery entirely, uninstall the app on GitHub instead.</p>
</section>

<section id="moving">
  <h2>Moving a repository between workspaces</h2>
  <p>Remove it from the first workspace, then add it in the second — connecting the installation there first if needed.</p>
  <p>History stays with the workspace that recorded it. The destination workspace starts accumulating from the moment you add it, because Distilled does not backfill. If you need continuity of the numbers, keep the repository where its history lives.</p>
</section>

<section id="same-repo">
  <h2>The same repository in two workspaces</h2>
  <p>That's supported and fully independent. Each workspace keeps its own copy of the pull requests, deployments, environments and metrics for that repository, and each incoming GitHub event is recorded in every workspace tracking it. Your personal workspace and your company's workspace can both track the same repository without either affecting the other.</p>
</section>

